{#- The body of the page in one language. index.html.j2 wraps it, and computes
    token, sub_url, n_multi and n_test before including it. Adding a language
    means adding a sibling of this file and naming it in
    `subscription_page_langs` — nothing else. -#}
<h1>Subscription</h1>

<p>Paste this link into your client as a <em>subscription</em> — configs will keep themselves up to date.</p>
<div class="row">
  <code>{{ sub_url }}</code>
  <button onclick="copy(this)">Copy</button>
</div>

<p><small>
  Clients: v2rayNG or Happ (Android), Streisand or Shadowrocket (iOS),
  Nekoray or v2rayN (desktop).
</small></p>

<h2>What is inside</h2>
<ul>
{% if n_multi > 0 %}
  <li><b>{{ n_multi }} two-hop route{{ 's' if n_multi > 1 else '' }}</b> — traffic
      leaves the network somewhere other than where it entered. Use these;
      if one is slow, try another.</li>
{% endif %}
{% if n_test > 0 %}
  <li><b>{{ n_test }} single-hop route{{ 's' if n_test > 1 else '' }}</b> — for
      testing only: the exit address is the entry address.</li>
{% endif %}
  <li>Every route ships twice, as <b>h2</b> and <b>h3</b>. Same route either
      way; the transport differs, so pick by what you are doing:
    <ul>
      <li><b>h2</b> — the default. Steadier where UDP is throttled, which is
          most networks here, and markedly faster on large downloads.</li>
      <li><b>h3</b> — for video and calls. A lost packet does not stall
          everything behind it and moving between Wi-Fi and mobile does not
          drop the connection, so 4K tends to play more smoothly. If h3 will
          not connect or keeps dropping, go back to h2.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>If a route will not connect at all, try the same route on the other
      transport: h2 and h3 run over different protocols, and a network
      interfering with one often lets the other through.</li>
</ul>
